# Tessera stdlib: values from text
#
# Text becomes a value the way any value becomes another type, with to[T]: text.to[S32]() is the number the text
# writes, and it crashes when the text isn't one. text.to_result[S32]() is the same conversion with the failure in a
# Result, for text that may be wrong (input, a file). A type that can be read from text conforms to Parsable[T] by
# defining that conversion for itself, routine Span[Byte].to_result[T], and text.to[T]() comes with it. Every integer
# type (Standard/IntConvert.te) and every float type (Standard/FloatConvert.te) is Parsable.

module Standard/Format

### Why text didn't convert to a value: `Empty` when there is nothing to read, `Invalid` when the text isn't written the way the type's values are, and `OutOfRange` when it names a value the type can't hold. Floats use only `Empty` and `Invalid`: a float too large for its type reads as an infinity.
choice ParseError: U8
    EMPTY  # no text, or a sign with no digits after it
    INVALID  # something the type's grammar doesn't allow
    OUT_OF_RANGE  # a value the type can't hold

### Types that can be read from text: `text.to[T]()` crashes on bad text, and `text.to_result[T]()` returns why. A type conforms by defining the conversion from Span[Byte] for itself, `routine Span[Byte].to_result[T](me: Span[Byte]) -> Result[T, ParseError]`.
### :typeparam T: The type read from text.
protocol Parsable[T]
needs T: typename
    ### The text as a T, or why it isn't one.
    ### :returns: The value, or a {ParseError}.
    routine Span[Byte].to_result[T](me: Span[Byte]) -> Result[T, ParseError]

obeys Parsable[S8], Parsable[S16], Parsable[S32], Parsable[S64], Parsable[S128], Parsable[S256]
obeys Parsable[U8], Parsable[U16], Parsable[U32], Parsable[U64], Parsable[U128], Parsable[U256]
obeys Parsable[SSize], Parsable[USize]
obeys Parsable[B16], Parsable[BF16], Parsable[B32], Parsable[B64], Parsable[B128]

### The text as a T. A type's own rules say which text is one: decimal digits with an optional sign for an integer,
### decimal or `inf` / `nan` for a float.
### :typeparam T: The type to read, a Parsable one.
### :returns: The value the text writes.
### :throws: Crashes with `InvalidValueError`, naming the text and the type, when the text isn't a T. Use
### `to_result[T]()` for text that may be wrong.
@track_caller
routine Span[Byte].to[T](me: Me) -> T
needs T: typename, Parsable[T]
    converted: Result[T, ParseError] = me.to_result[T]()
    when converted
        is T as value => return value
        is ParseError as error => crash_conversion(text: me, target: type_name[T](), error)

### Crashes with `InvalidValueError` for text that isn't a value of a type: `Can't convert "12a" to S32: the text
### isn't a valid S32.` Text past 64 bytes is cut short, with "..." after it.
### :param text: The text that didn't convert.
### :param target: The name of the type it was converted to.
### :param error: Why it didn't.
@noreturn
@track_caller
private routine crash_conversion(text: Span[Byte], target: Span[Byte], error: ParseError) -> None
    local buffer: @Array[Byte, 400] <- .hollow()
    local message: @SliceWriter <- .construct(data: buffer.to[@Byte](), capacity: 400)

    message.write("Can't convert ")
    if text.count() > 64
        shown: Span[Byte] = text.slice(start: 0, end: 64)
        message.write("{shown.diagnose()}...")
    else
        message.write("{text.diagnose()}")
    message.write(" to {target}: ")
    when error
        ParseError.EMPTY => message.write("the text is empty.")
        ParseError.INVALID => message.write("the text isn't a valid {target}.")
        ParseError.OUT_OF_RANGE => message.write("the value is out of {target}'s range.")
    crash(name: "InvalidValueError", message: message.getview())
